  ],
  "details": "<p><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1229\">Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>\u00a0in 2006 as part of the \"Crac des Chevaliers and Qal\u2019at Salah El-Din\" listing under criteria ii and iv of UNESCO's World Heritage List\u00a0<a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://whc.unesco.org/en/criteria/\">Operational Guidelines</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The site's specific significance include:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>The Crac des Chevaliers, which was built by the Hospitaller Order of Saint John of Jerusalem from <strong>1142 to 1271 AD</strong></li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>The first phase of construction was carried out between <strong>1142 and 1190 AD</strong></li>\r\n<li>The second phase of construction was carried out between <strong>1190 and 1200 AD</strong></li>\r\n<li>The third phase of construction was carried out by the <strong>Mamluks</strong> between <strong>1200 and 1271 AD</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>It ranks among the best-preserved examples of<strong>\u00a0<strong>Crusader castles</strong></strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</ul>",
